1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Subtitles added by Hanson Hu

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Episode 16 - Full of Surprises

3
00:02:00,840 --> 00:02:02,680
The mailman just dropped some mail in our box, Grandpa.

4
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:05,860
Probably a lot of advertising and bills.

5
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:08,460
Why don't you write to me, Robbie,

6
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:10,360
so I can get some interesting mail?

7
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:33,280
You were right, Grandpa.

8
00:02:34,620 --> 00:02:38,560
Advertising, bills, bills, advertising.

9
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:41,680
Yes. It's just like I said, Robbie.

10
00:02:41,720 --> 00:02:43,600
Nothing interesting.

11
00:02:43,860 --> 00:02:45,680
You won't believe it, Grandpa,

12
00:02:45,680 --> 00:02:48,000
but there's a letter here addressed to you,

13
00:02:48,300 --> 00:02:51,280
Mr. Malcolm Stewart, and it looks like a personal letter.

14
00:02:51,740 --> 00:02:52,540
Oh, it must be a bill.

15
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:54,140
I don't think so.

16
00:02:54,360 --> 00:02:55,960
The return name and address is

17
00:02:55,960 --> 00:02:59,440
Pete Waters, RFD Number 1, Chesterton.

18
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:01,220
You're joking. Pete Waters?

19
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:06,360
Pete Waters, RFD Number 1, Chesterton.

20
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:07,880
You know him?

21
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:10,240
Do I know Pete Waters?

22
00:03:10,240 --> 00:03:12,000
You bet I do!

23
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:14,520
He was my roommate in college.

24
00:03:20,040 --> 00:03:22,640
He visited with Grandma and me in Florida

25
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:23,700
about five years ago.

26
00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:31,820
What does he say?

27
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:32,820
Is he OK?

28
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:35,000
Yeah, he's fine.

29
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:36,020
Just fine.

30
00:03:37,380 --> 00:03:39,540
He's writing to invite me

31
00:03:39,540 --> 00:03:42,520
to spend a weekend with him at his farm.

32
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:45,080
He's planning a get-together

33
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:46,700
with two or three other college friends.

34
00:03:47,780 --> 00:03:52,300
A kind of a fifty-year anniversary reunion.

35
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:54,040
Sounds like fun.

36
00:03:54,520 --> 00:03:56,820
Fifty years? Wow!

37
00:03:57,140 --> 00:03:59,120
It sounds like fun to me, too, Robbie.

38
00:03:59,580 --> 00:04:01,480
What kind of farm does he have?

39
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:03,840
I've never been there, Robbie,

40
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:07,920
but he has chickens and cows and all.

41
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:11,320
That means fresh eggs and fresh milk.

42
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:12,900
Does he have a family?

43
00:04:13,740 --> 00:04:15,200
No, he doesn't, Robbie.

44
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:16,560
He never married.

45
00:04:17,320 --> 00:04:18,000
He's not as lucky as I am

46
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:22,100
to have a family and grandchildren.

47
00:04:23,460 --> 00:04:24,960
I'm a lucky man.

48
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:27,080
How come he never got married?

49
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:29,360
That's a good question, Robbie.

50
00:04:29,580 --> 00:04:31,340
A very good question.

51
00:04:32,440 --> 00:04:34,200
He never married because

52
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:37,540
the girl he was in love with in college married someone else.

53
00:04:37,840 --> 00:04:39,200
As simple as that.

54
00:04:39,840 --> 00:04:41,160
He never got over it.

55
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:43,840
He must have loved her very much.

56
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:45,880
Yes. Very much.

57
00:04:47,040 --> 00:04:48,360
Lillian Winters.

58
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:50,160
She was in our class.

59
00:04:50,540 --> 00:04:51,520
And what happened?

60
00:04:52,060 --> 00:04:54,200
She was in love with Donald McGrath,

61
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:56,520
the quarterback on our football team.

62
00:04:57,120 --> 00:04:59,920
Football players are always popular with the ladies.

63
00:05:00,020 --> 00:05:04,200
She liked Pete, and they went to dances together.

64
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:06,920
But her heart was with Donald.

65
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:08,920
Did he ever get over it?

66
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:10,780
No, he never did.

67
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:12,320
Where is she today?

68
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:14,760
I don't know.

69
00:05:15,740 --> 00:05:17,900
Maybe Lillian will be at the reunion.

70
00:05:18,120 --> 00:05:19,280
You think so?

71
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:21,220
Pete's full of surprises.

72
00:05:21,220 --> 00:05:23,500
I wish I could go there with you, Grandpa.

73
00:05:23,980 --> 00:05:25,640
What do you think the surprise will be?

74
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:29,520
With Pete, you never know, Robbie.

75
00:05:29,920 --> 00:05:33,760
Won't it be exciting to see all your college friends there again?

76
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:35,440
It is already.

77
00:05:36,520 --> 00:05:39,060
I'm kind of excited about going now.

78
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:43,080
Next weekend...

79
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:46,480
sleep over Friday and Saturday night

80
00:05:46,720 --> 00:05:48,420
and come back Sunday.

81
00:05:49,880 --> 00:05:51,040
I can't wait!

82
00:05:53,580 --> 00:05:55,260
Don't you think you ought to call Pete

83
00:05:55,260 --> 00:05:56,320
and tell him you're coming?

84
00:05:56,560 --> 00:05:58,080
You're reading my mind, Robbie.

85
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:20,860
I got a letter in the mail today.

86
00:06:20,860 --> 00:06:23,220
It said, "How you doing, old friend?

87
00:06:23,420 --> 00:06:24,880
I've been thinking about you.

88
00:06:24,880 --> 00:06:26,440
So tell me what's new.

89
00:06:26,440 --> 00:06:28,560
We should get together again."

90
00:06:28,620 --> 00:06:31,640
I haven't seen you for a long time.

91
00:06:31,640 --> 00:06:34,240
I was thinking about you today.

92
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:37,220
And if I could, I'd come right over,

93
00:06:37,220 --> 00:06:39,080
but you live too far away.

94
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:44,800
The letter said, "Here's your invitation."

95
00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:50,760
It said, "We're having a celebration.

96
00:06:51,560 --> 00:06:56,380
Let's get together, have a reunion.

97
00:06:57,620 --> 00:06:58,660
It's been a while.

98
00:06:58,660 --> 00:07:00,240
You got to show me that smile,

99
00:07:00,280 --> 00:07:02,120
and I want to see you again.

100
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:06,560
Let's get together, have a reunion.

101
00:07:06,700 --> 00:07:09,540
Come on, you're invited.

102
00:07:09,540 --> 00:07:10,980
We'll be reunited,

103
00:07:10,980 --> 00:07:13,460
and we'll get to know each other again."

104
00:07:13,660 --> 00:07:16,540
I want to hear those old stories.

105
00:07:16,540 --> 00:07:18,400
I want to sing an old, old song.

106
00:07:18,520 --> 00:07:20,560
The letter said, "Bring your camera,

107
00:07:20,560 --> 00:07:21,760
take some pictures.

108
00:07:21,760 --> 00:07:24,080
Hope you can come along.

109
00:07:24,280 --> 00:07:27,120
And if you can come, won't you let me know?

110
00:07:27,120 --> 00:07:29,840
You can write or give me a call.

111
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:32,700
Before you know it, you're gonna feel

112
00:07:32,700 --> 00:07:34,540
like the years never passed at all."

113
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:40,160
The letter said, "Here's your invitation."

114
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:46,200
It said, "We're having a celebration.

115
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:51,140
Let's get together, have a reunion.

116
00:07:51,320 --> 00:07:54,080
Oh, yeah, it's been a while.

117
00:07:54,080 --> 00:07:55,540
You got to show me that smile,

118
00:07:55,540 --> 00:07:57,520
and I want to see you again.

119
00:07:57,720 --> 00:08:02,600
Let's get together, have a reunion."

120
00:09:20,400 --> 00:09:21,360
Hello. What can I do for you?

121
00:09:21,360 --> 00:09:23,120
Hi. Fill 'er up.

122
00:09:23,120 --> 00:09:24,360
I need a full tank.

123
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:25,480
Check the hood?

124
00:09:25,720 --> 00:09:26,680
No, thanks.

125
00:09:35,700 --> 00:09:36,960
Whereabouts are you headed?

126
00:09:37,200 --> 00:09:40,080
Pete Waters's farm, near Chesterton.

127
00:09:40,440 --> 00:09:41,580
I know it well.

128
00:09:42,120 --> 00:09:45,340
Pete Waters's lived around here almost as long as I have.

129
00:09:45,520 --> 00:09:48,640
How long is it going to take for me to get there?

130
00:09:48,800 --> 00:09:50,160
About fifteen minutes.

131
00:09:50,560 --> 00:09:52,260
There's a shortcut - if you know it.

132
00:09:52,880 --> 00:09:53,800
No, I don't.

133
00:09:53,800 --> 00:09:57,260
Could you tell me how to use the shortcut?

134
00:09:57,320 --> 00:09:59,720
Sure. You take the next left turn.

135
00:09:59,760 --> 00:10:01,720
You'll see a stop sign.

136
00:10:02,320 --> 00:10:04,080
Make a right at the stop sign.

137
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:07,720
Stay on that road, and you'll cross a blue bridge.

138
00:10:08,080 --> 00:10:10,360
Then you'll see a big old red barn.

139
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:12,480
That's the back of Pete Waters's place.

140
00:10:14,840 --> 00:10:18,040
That's quite a difference from the directions that Pete sent me.

141
00:10:19,980 --> 00:10:23,400
Now, if you take that route, it's probably a lot simpler,

142
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:25,300
but it'll take you ten minutes longer.

143
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:31,300
OK. Let me repeat it.

144
00:10:32,460 --> 00:10:35,160
I take the next left turn to the stop sign.

145
00:10:35,280 --> 00:10:39,520
Then a right across a blue bridge,

146
00:10:39,580 --> 00:10:42,800
and then a big red barn.

147
00:10:42,800 --> 00:10:43,780
Can't miss it.

148
00:10:43,780 --> 00:10:48,720
How much do I owe you?

149
00:10:48,780 --> 00:10:53,200
Well, that'll be eighteen dollars and seventy cents.

150
00:10:53,200 --> 00:10:55,440
No charge for the cleanup.

151
00:10:55,440 --> 00:10:56,240
It's on the house.

152
00:10:58,880 --> 00:10:59,920
Well, here's a twenty.

153
00:11:00,020 --> 00:11:08,760
Ah. OK. That's a dollar and thirty cents change.

154
00:11:09,460 --> 00:11:12,540
There we go... will make twenty. Thanks.

155
00:11:14,160 --> 00:11:15,080
Thank you.

156
00:11:15,440 --> 00:11:17,320
And thanks for the directions.

157
00:11:55,880 --> 00:11:57,720
This must be Pete's barn.

158
00:11:58,920 --> 00:12:00,600
Turn right to the house.

159
00:12:05,980 --> 00:12:06,880
Hi.

160
00:12:12,500 --> 00:12:13,320
Hi. What can I do for you?

161
00:12:13,680 --> 00:12:15,200
Is this the Pete Waters farm?

162
00:12:15,200 --> 00:12:16,240
It is.

163
00:12:16,480 --> 00:12:17,540
I'm a friend of Pete's.

164
00:12:17,760 --> 00:12:18,480
I'm looking for the house.

165
00:12:18,740 --> 00:12:19,820
It's just over yonder.

166
00:12:19,880 --> 00:12:21,120
Keep along this road

167
00:12:21,120 --> 00:12:22,200
till you get to the end of the fence.

168
00:12:22,200 --> 00:12:23,200
You'll see the chicken house.

169
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:24,480
His house is on the left.

170
00:12:24,500 --> 00:12:25,740
Well, thanks.

171
00:12:56,960 --> 00:12:59,180
"Have gone to the railroad station.

172
00:12:59,680 --> 00:13:01,960
Back soon with a surprise.

173
00:13:02,040 --> 00:13:03,880
Make yourself at home.

174
00:13:04,280 --> 00:13:06,040
Have a look around. Pete."

175
00:13:08,520 --> 00:13:10,440
Same old Pete Waters.

176
00:13:10,920 --> 00:13:12,920
Always full of surprises.

177
00:13:23,240 --> 00:13:26,880
Could you tell me how to use the shortcut?

178
00:13:26,960 --> 00:13:29,360
Sure. You take the next left turn.

179
00:13:29,860 --> 00:13:31,280
You'll see a stop sign.

180
00:13:31,960 --> 00:13:33,640
Make a right at the stop sign.

181
00:13:34,100 --> 00:13:37,420
Stay on that road, and you'll cross a blue bridge.

182
00:13:37,720 --> 00:13:40,080
Then you'll see a big old red barn.

183
00:13:41,600 --> 00:13:45,800
Take the next left turn. You'll see a stop sign.

184
00:13:46,520 --> 00:13:48,120
Make a right at the stop sign.

185
00:13:49,060 --> 00:13:53,180
Stay on that road, and you'll cross a blue bridge.

186
00:13:54,080 --> 00:13:56,480
Then you'll see a big old red barn.

187
00:13:57,460 --> 00:13:57,960
Here goes.

188
00:13:59,020 --> 00:14:01,640
Oh, oh, here. Yes. OK.

189
00:14:03,340 --> 00:14:07,100
Well, sir, keep along this road

190
00:14:07,100 --> 00:14:08,200
until you get to the end of the fence.

191
00:14:08,200 --> 00:14:09,160
You'll see the chicken house.

192
00:14:09,160 --> 00:14:10,800
His house is on the left.

193
00:14:12,080 --> 00:14:13,240
All right.

194
00:14:15,160 --> 00:14:18,860
Well, I made it to Pete's house, but he's not here.

195
00:14:19,100 --> 00:14:22,520
I think I'd like to see some more of the countryside

196
00:14:22,520 --> 00:14:24,040
while I wait for him to get back.

197
00:14:24,680 --> 00:14:28,440
I'll back out of the driveway and be on my way.

198
00:14:29,040 --> 00:14:32,020
Dead end.

199
00:14:33,120 --> 00:14:35,680
Let me back up again and go in the opposite direction

200
00:14:35,680 --> 00:14:36,960
and see what I find.

201
00:14:39,640 --> 00:14:41,700
What a nice horse farm.

202
00:14:42,640 --> 00:14:44,880
Well, I better get back to Pete's.

203
00:14:45,520 --> 00:14:46,260
Let's see.

204
00:14:46,480 --> 00:14:49,320
All I have to do is back out of the driveway,

205
00:14:49,700 --> 00:14:51,720
make a right past the lake,

206
00:14:51,720 --> 00:14:53,200
go to the fork in the road,

207
00:14:53,440 --> 00:14:54,760
cross the railroad tracks,

208
00:14:55,080 --> 00:14:57,360
and make a right at the first intersection.

209
00:14:58,520 --> 00:15:00,620
Pete's driveway should be the first right turn.

210
00:15:01,720 --> 00:15:02,400
I made it!

211
00:16:33,160 --> 00:16:34,120
Don't tell me.

212
00:16:34,280 --> 00:16:35,860
Please don't tell me.

213
00:16:36,280 --> 00:16:38,120
I recognize you...

214
00:16:38,120 --> 00:16:39,320
Oh, don't be silly, Arnie.

215
00:16:39,320 --> 00:16:40,960
Of course you recognize him.

216
00:16:40,960 --> 00:16:43,420
Except for the beard, he hasn't changed in fifty years.

217
00:16:43,420 --> 00:16:44,900
It's Malcolm Stewart!

218
00:16:44,900 --> 00:16:46,880
I know it's Malcolm Stewart.

219
00:16:46,880 --> 00:16:49,340
You haven't changed much in fifty years.

220
00:16:50,040 --> 00:16:53,000
Peggy... Peggy Pendleton!

221
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:54,560
You're Peggy Pendleton!

222
00:16:54,740 --> 00:16:56,120
Who am I, you old rascal?

223
00:16:56,120 --> 00:16:57,160
You don't recognize me, do you?

224
00:16:59,280 --> 00:17:00,840
I know who you are.

225
00:17:00,840 --> 00:17:02,760
You're Arnold Franklin!

226
00:17:02,760 --> 00:17:04,560
I know who you are!

227
00:17:04,560 --> 00:17:07,400
You look wonderful, Malcolm!

228
00:17:13,240 --> 00:17:14,200
Sit down. Sit down.

229
00:17:14,760 --> 00:17:15,700
Pete isn't home.

230
00:17:15,700 --> 00:17:16,320
Really?

231
00:17:18,060 --> 00:17:19,820
No. He left a note on the door

232
00:17:19,820 --> 00:17:23,000
saying he was going to the railroad station

233
00:17:23,060 --> 00:17:24,600
to pick up a surprise.

234
00:17:24,680 --> 00:17:26,120
He's so funny.

235
00:17:26,120 --> 00:17:27,300
Always full of surprises,

236
00:17:27,300 --> 00:17:28,840
even fifty years later.

237
00:17:30,240 --> 00:17:32,640
The two of you look unbelievable!

238
00:17:32,640 --> 00:17:34,700
How's your family?

239
00:17:34,720 --> 00:17:36,020
Oh, I was sorry

240
00:17:36,240 --> 00:17:37,000
to hear about your wife having passed away.

241
00:17:38,380 --> 00:17:40,220
Yes. About four years ago.

242
00:17:40,220 --> 00:17:42,840
And you're living with your children now? In New York?

243
00:17:42,840 --> 00:17:44,240
Pete wrote us and told us.

244
00:17:44,240 --> 00:17:45,040
Yup.

245
00:17:47,980 --> 00:17:49,400
Retired and moved to New York

246
00:17:49,440 --> 00:17:52,000
to live with my son and his family.

247
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:52,920
By the way,

248
00:17:53,040 --> 00:17:55,000
what do you think this big surprise is?

249
00:17:55,560 --> 00:17:57,520
It could be most anything, knowing Pete.

250
00:18:00,680 --> 00:18:02,360
Hey, that must be Pete!

251
00:18:03,120 --> 00:18:05,120
Now we'll find out about the surprise.

252
00:18:08,640 --> 00:18:10,400
So good to see you all!

253
00:18:10,400 --> 00:18:11,880
Oh, Pete, Pete!

254
00:18:11,880 --> 00:18:14,060
Oh, it's so good to see you!

255
00:18:16,160 --> 00:18:17,840
Remember Lillian?

256
00:18:17,960 --> 00:18:19,520
I remember all of you.

257
00:18:19,560 --> 00:18:20,960
You haven't changed a bit.

258
00:18:22,220 --> 00:18:23,880
Lillian! Oh, my gosh!

259
00:18:23,880 --> 00:18:24,280
Lillian Winters.

260
00:18:24,280 --> 00:18:27,360
We were together in the Thursday night drama society.

261
00:18:28,320 --> 00:18:29,740
Remember me, Malcolm?

262
00:18:29,740 --> 00:18:32,000
Oh, beautiful as ever, Lillian.

263
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:33,520
How's Donald?

264
00:18:34,960 --> 00:18:36,080
That's OK, Malcolm.

265
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:38,840
Donald passed away a couple of years ago.

266
00:18:38,840 --> 00:18:39,240
Sorry to hear that, Lillian.

267
00:18:39,240 --> 00:18:40,280
Oh, I'm so sorry.

268
00:18:40,280 --> 00:18:42,920
I would not have missed this get-together for the world!

269
00:18:42,980 --> 00:18:44,880
And your little surprise, Pete?

270
00:18:45,200 --> 00:18:46,800
You really surprised me

271
00:18:46,800 --> 00:18:48,760
by having us all come together.

272
00:18:49,340 --> 00:18:52,660
You don't know what the surprise is yet?

273
00:18:52,800 --> 00:18:54,240
Come on!

274
00:18:55,220 --> 00:18:56,780
We'll tell you the big surprise.

275
00:19:02,120 --> 00:19:05,720
I've invited you here for the weekend to help celebrate.

276
00:19:05,720 --> 00:19:06,560
Celebrate?

277
00:19:06,740 --> 00:19:08,220
Our fiftieth reunion?

278
00:19:08,220 --> 00:19:09,000
No.

279
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:10,880
No. Pete wants to tell you...

280
00:19:10,880 --> 00:19:13,360
Now, let me have the honor, Lillian.

281
00:19:13,440 --> 00:19:15,680
For goodness' sake, Pete, tell us!

282
00:19:15,680 --> 00:19:17,160
I can't wait much longer.

283
00:19:18,960 --> 00:19:21,860
Well, I am pouring this iced tea

284
00:19:21,860 --> 00:19:25,760
so that we can toast Lillian and me.

285
00:19:26,640 --> 00:19:28,860
You don't mean to tell me that you and...

286
00:19:28,860 --> 00:19:30,600
Yes, I do.

287
00:19:30,920 --> 00:19:34,480
I have loved Lillian all these years,

288
00:19:34,700 --> 00:19:37,600
so I asked her to be Mrs. Pete Waters.

289
00:19:39,280 --> 00:19:40,900
And I said yes.

290
00:19:41,020 --> 00:19:44,040
I knew it! I knew it!

291
00:19:44,440 --> 00:19:44,920
Congratulations!

292
00:19:44,920 --> 00:19:46,480
Oh, Lillian,

293
00:19:46,480 --> 00:19:48,200
I am so happy for you both.

294
00:19:48,200 --> 00:19:49,080
It's wonderful!

295
00:19:49,800 --> 00:19:51,160
It's wonderful for me.

296
00:19:51,340 --> 00:19:54,720
Lillian will make me a happy man, finally.

297
00:19:54,840 --> 00:19:57,180
You are full of surprises, Pete.

298
00:19:57,180 --> 00:19:59,460
We are going to

299
00:19:59,460 --> 00:20:04,060
spend the entire weekend having a good time together here

300
00:20:04,060 --> 00:20:04,780
on the farm.

301
00:20:04,780 --> 00:20:07,600
We are going to celebrate all weekend.

302
00:20:07,600 --> 00:20:09,040
When is the wedding?

303
00:20:09,040 --> 00:20:11,840
That's another surprise.

304
00:20:12,040 --> 00:20:15,440
Lillian and I were married two weeks ago in Detroit.

305
00:20:15,440 --> 00:20:18,520
She's come here to stay.

306
00:20:18,800 --> 00:20:20,700
Wait till I tell my family about this!

307
00:20:20,800 --> 00:20:22,860
Why, that's wonderful!

308
00:20:31,200 --> 00:20:33,800
What a weekend!

309
00:20:34,640 --> 00:20:37,040
Pete and Lillian seemed so happy

310
00:20:37,940 --> 00:20:40,300
after all these years.

311
00:20:40,300 --> 00:20:42,600
Fifty years, to be exact.

312
00:20:42,800 --> 00:20:46,640
Fifty years ago, I was a young man.

313
00:20:47,560 --> 00:20:49,120
Not bad-looking either.

314
00:20:49,620 --> 00:20:53,640
And Pete was my college roommate.

315
00:20:53,640 --> 00:20:55,200
A real pal.

316
00:20:55,680 --> 00:20:57,740
He conducted the orchestra.

317
00:20:58,080 --> 00:21:00,060
He really loved music.

318
00:21:01,960 --> 00:21:04,640
Pete loved Lillian from the moment they met.

319
00:21:04,960 --> 00:21:07,860
Sometimes he'd call the radio station where I was an announcer

320
00:21:08,040 --> 00:21:11,020
and ask me to play certain love songs for Lillian.

321
00:21:11,680 --> 00:21:13,640
She's always been lovely.

322
00:21:14,360 --> 00:21:16,600
And he wanted to spend his life with her.

323
00:21:16,600 --> 00:21:18,340
Lill liked Pete,

324
00:21:18,740 --> 00:21:20,920
but she fell in love with Donald.

325
00:21:22,300 --> 00:21:24,720
Donald and I were on the Michigan football team.

326
00:21:24,720 --> 00:21:27,240
He was the quarterback and captain,

327
00:21:27,240 --> 00:21:30,760
and I was the smallest one on the team.

328
00:21:31,600 --> 00:21:34,560
I finally got to play my senior year.

329
00:21:36,320 --> 00:21:38,720
Lill and Donald got married right after college

330
00:21:38,720 --> 00:21:40,520
and bought a house in Detroit, Michigan.

331
00:21:41,960 --> 00:21:44,280
Oh, look at this.

332
00:21:44,740 --> 00:21:47,640
Here's a picture of Lill and their son, Donald Jr.

333
00:21:48,940 --> 00:21:53,000
Later, the family grew to include Robert and Barbara.

334
00:21:54,320 --> 00:21:56,520
Meanwhile, Pete stayed single,

335
00:21:56,980 --> 00:21:58,940
working hard, conducting orchestras,

336
00:21:59,200 --> 00:22:01,500
playing in bands and teaching music,

337
00:22:03,020 --> 00:22:06,600
until he retired and bought the farm in Chesterton four years ago.

338
00:22:06,640 --> 00:22:09,040
Donald passed away,

339
00:22:10,220 --> 00:22:14,560
and now Pete and Lill are married and living on his farm.

340
00:22:16,440 --> 00:22:19,040
You never know what's going to happen.

341
00:22:20,020 --> 00:22:21,800
Life is full of surprises.

